// @check
// @error cannot assign i32 | "err" to 'z' of type i32
// @error cannot assign Circle | "err" to 'w' of type Circle
// @warning Unused variable `z` (prefix with `_` to suppress, or remove it)
// @warning Unused variable `w` (prefix with `_` to suppress, or remove it)
// THE HALF OF D1049 THAT MUST NOT MOVE — the instantiations where the substituted union is
// a GENUINE two-arm box and the subsumed-arm collapse must not fire.
//
// `"err"`'s base is `string`, and `string` is an arm of neither `i32 | "err"` nor
// `Circle | "err"`, so each of those adds a value and keeps both arms. A collapse that ate
// them would be silent at the construct position, which is why this is pinned as a checker
// fact: while the `"err"` arm survives, `pick(7)` is not assignable to `i32`.
//
// IT STAYS A `@check` FIXTURE NOW THAT D1042 HAS CLOSED, and the reason changed. It used to
// be forced: a boxed union minted at the pin had no `is` lowering at all
// (`emitProgram: union box atom test on a union with no recorded members: i32|"err"`), so no
// running control could discriminate its arms. That refusal is gone — the pin registers its
// union through canon — and the RUNNING discrimination now lives in
// `pin-union-return-registers-through-canon.vl`, at both `T = i32` and `T = Circle`. What
// belongs HERE is the half a running program cannot state: an ASSIGNMENT that must stay a type
// error. `pick(7)` runs and is `i32 | "err"`; the fact this file pins is that it is still not
// assignable to `i32`, which is only true while the `"err"` arm survives the collapse.

type Circle = { r: i32 }

function pick<T>(v: T): T | "err" { return v }

const z: i32 = pick(7)

const c: Circle = { r: 3 }
const w: Circle = pick(c)
